This version writes the tree level by level. Each real node adds its left and right children to the queue, including missing children as `null`. That makes the parent-child positions clear during decoding.

A quick dry run for a root `5` with only a left child `3` gives `5,3,null,null,null`. The decoder reads `5`, then attaches `3` on the left and leaves the right side empty.

```java
import java.util.LinkedList;
import java.util.Queue;

/**
 * Definition for a binary tree node.
 * public class TreeNode {
 *     int data;
 *     TreeNode left;
 *     TreeNode right;
 *     TreeNode(int val) { data = val; left = null; right = null; }
 * }
 **/

class Solution {
    public String serialize(TreeNode root) {
        if (root == null) {
            return "";
        }

        StringBuilder result = new StringBuilder();
        Queue<TreeNode> queue = new LinkedList<>();
        queue.offer(root);

        while (!queue.isEmpty()) {
            TreeNode node = queue.poll();
            if (node == null) {
                result.append("null,");
                continue;
            }

            result.append(node.data).append(',');
            queue.offer(node.left);
            queue.offer(node.right);
        }

        return result.toString();
    }

    public TreeNode deserialize(String data) {
        if (data == null || data.isEmpty()) {
            return null;
        }

        String[] values = data.split(",");
        if (values.length == 0 || values[0].equals("null")) {
            return null;
        }

        TreeNode root = new TreeNode(Integer.parseInt(values[0]));
        Queue<TreeNode> queue = new LinkedList<>();
        queue.offer(root);
        int index = 1;

        while (!queue.isEmpty() && index < values.length) {
            TreeNode parent = queue.poll();

            if (index < values.length && !values[index].equals("null")) {
                parent.left = new TreeNode(Integer.parseInt(values[index]));
                queue.offer(parent.left);
            }
            index++;

            if (index < values.length && !values[index].equals("null")) {
                parent.right = new TreeNode(Integer.parseInt(values[index]));
                queue.offer(parent.right);
            }
            index++;
        }

        return root;
    }
}
```

The queue is a `LinkedList` because it can store null entries while serializing. An empty input string represents a null root.

Time complexity: `O(n)` for serialization and deserialization.
Space complexity: `O(n)` for the queue and encoded data.

Level order is convenient for this problem because every node is followed by its two child positions. The encoded list keeps `null` entries, so the decoder always knows which position is missing.

For a tree with root `7`, left child `4`, and right child `9`, the output starts as `7,4,9,null,null,null,null`. A nil root returns an empty string.

```go
import (
	"strconv"
	"strings"
)

/**
 * Definition for a binary tree node.
 * type TreeNode struct {
 *     Data int
 *     Left *TreeNode
 *     Right *TreeNode
 * }
 * func NewTreeNode(val int) *TreeNode {
 *     return &TreeNode{Data: val}
 * }
 */

func serialize(root *TreeNode) string {
	if root == nil {
		return ""
	}

	values := make([]string, 0)
	queue := []*TreeNode{root}

	for head := 0; head < len(queue); head++ {
		node := queue[head]
		if node == nil {
			values = append(values, "null")
			continue
		}

		values = append(values, strconv.Itoa(node.Data))
		queue = append(queue, node.Left, node.Right)
	}

	return strings.Join(values, ",")
}

func deserialize(data string) *TreeNode {
	if data == "" {
		return nil
	}

	values := strings.Split(data, ",")
	if values[0] == "null" {
		return nil
	}

	rootValue, err := strconv.Atoi(values[0])
	if err != nil {
		return nil
	}

	root := NewTreeNode(rootValue)
	parents := []*TreeNode{root}
	index := 1

	for head := 0; head < len(parents) && index < len(values); head++ {
		parent := parents[head]

		if index < len(values) && values[index] != "null" {
			value, parseErr := strconv.Atoi(values[index])
			if parseErr == nil {
				parent.Left = NewTreeNode(value)
				parents = append(parents, parent.Left)
			}
		}
		index++

		if index < len(values) && values[index] != "null" {
			value, parseErr := strconv.Atoi(values[index])
			if parseErr == nil {
				parent.Right = NewTreeNode(value)
				parents = append(parents, parent.Right)
			}
		}
		index++
	}

	return root
}
```

The queue slice uses a head index, so elements are not repeatedly removed from the front. The original tree is only read during serialization.

Time complexity: `O(n)` for both functions.
Space complexity: `O(n)` for the queue and encoded values.

I used a simple depth first format here. A number means a real node and `N` means no node. Since the values and missing children are written in the same order, decoding can consume the list from left to right.

This also works when values are negative, because the number conversion is done only for tokens that are not `N`.

```csharp
using System;
using System.Collections.Generic;

public class TreeNode {
    public int val;
    public TreeNode left;
    public TreeNode right;
    public TreeNode(int val = 0, TreeNode left = null, TreeNode right = null) {
        this.val = val;
        this.left = left;
        this.right = right;
    }
}

public class Solution {
    public string serialize(TreeNode root) {
        List<string> values = new List<string>();
        Write(root, values);
        return string.Join(",", values);
    }

    private void Write(TreeNode node, List<string> values) {
        if (node == null) {
            values.Add("N");
            return;
        }

        values.Add(node.val.ToString());
        Write(node.left, values);
        Write(node.right, values);
    }

    public TreeNode deserialize(string data) {
        if (string.IsNullOrEmpty(data)) {
            return null;
        }

        string[] values = data.Split(',');
        int index = 0;
        return Read(values, ref index);
    }

    private TreeNode Read(string[] values, ref int index) {
        if (index >= values.Length || values[index] == "N") {
            index++;
            return null;
        }

        TreeNode node = new TreeNode(int.Parse(values[index]));
        index++;
        node.left = Read(values, ref index);
        node.right = Read(values, ref index);
        return node;
    }
}
```

For `null`, serialization returns `N`, and decoding returns null without trying to read a number. The recursive depth can reach the number of nodes for a very one-sided tree.

Time complexity: `O(n)` for serialization and deserialization.
Space complexity: `O(n)` for the output and recursive calls.

The string keeps a preorder sequence and writes `#` whenever a child is missing. During deserialization, one shared position reads exactly one token for each tree position.

The important part is that null children are stored too. Without them, a left-only child and a right-only child could produce the same sequence.

```cpp
#include <sstream>
#include <string>
#include <vector>
using namespace std;

/**
 * Definition for a binary tree node.
 * struct TreeNode {
 *     int data;
 *     TreeNode *left;
 *     TreeNode *right;
 *     TreeNode(int val) : data(val), left(nullptr), right(nullptr) {}
 * };
 **/

class Solution {
public:
    string serialize(TreeNode* root) {
        vector<string> tokens;
        vector<TreeNode*> stack;
        stack.push_back(root);

        while (!stack.empty()) {
            TreeNode* node = stack.back();
            stack.pop_back();

            if (node == nullptr) {
                tokens.push_back("#");
                continue;
            }

            tokens.push_back(to_string(node->data));
            stack.push_back(node->right);
            stack.push_back(node->left);
        }

        string result;
        for (int i = 0; i < static_cast<int>(tokens.size()); i++) {
            if (i > 0) {
                result += ' ';
            }
            result += tokens[i];
        }
        return result;
    }

    TreeNode* deserialize(string data) {
        if (data.empty()) {
            return nullptr;
        }

        istringstream input(data);
        vector<string> tokens;
        string token;
        while (input >> token) {
            tokens.push_back(token);
        }

        int index = 0;
        return build(tokens, index);
    }

private:
    TreeNode* build(const vector<string>& tokens, int& index) {
        if (index >= static_cast<int>(tokens.size()) || tokens[index] == "#") {
            index++;
            return nullptr;
        }

        TreeNode* node = new TreeNode(stoi(tokens[index]));
        index++;
        node->left = build(tokens, index);
        node->right = build(tokens, index);
        return node;
    }
};
```

For a single node, the stream is `value # #`, so both recursive child calls stop immediately. The input tree is not changed.

Time complexity: `O(n)` for each method.
Space complexity: `O(n)` for the token list, stack, and recursion.

